# Instinct disclosure patterns: the private names to keep out of a shared pack.
#
# Copy this file to ~/.claude/instinct/disclosure-patterns.txt (or point the
# INSTINCT_DISCLOSURE_PATTERNS environment variable at another file) and list YOUR
# names below. `python3 scripts/instinct.py export --shareable` refuses to write a
# pack if any instinct's file name, name, description or text matches a line here,
# and tells you which file and which pattern. Nothing is read from this repo: a
# list of the names you want kept private does not belong in a public project, so
# it stays on your machine.
#
# Format:
#   - One regular expression per line, matched case-insensitively.
#   - Lines starting with # and blank lines are ignored. Put every comment on its
#     own line: a # after a pattern would be read as part of the pattern, so the
#     export stops with an error instead of quietly never matching.
#   - A pattern that matches everything, such as one with an empty alternative
#     (acme|), is refused too.
#   - Already built in for everyone, so do not list it: links to private session
#     artifacts (claude.ai/code/artifact).
#
# Tips:
#   - A word-boundary pattern such as \bacme\b does NOT match inside a longer word,
#     so "acmeplan" slips past it. Give each compound form its own line.
#   - Wrap short names in \b...\b so they do not match inside unrelated words.
#
# This file ships with no active patterns, so a fresh install checks only for
# private session links. Remove the leading # from the examples below and replace
# them with your own.
#
# People:
# \b(Jane Doe|Janey)\b
#
# Companies, clients and products:
# \bacme\b
# acmeplan
#
# Places:
# \bspringfield\b
#
# Ticket prefixes:
# ACME-\d+
